# Intent: tell apart the ways the camera can fail to send (host log 2026-10-21: the avatar socket connects and the host asks for a camera stream, yet no frame arrives). The minute "camera" event now also reports encode failures apart from capture failures, how many binary messages the avatar socket received and the type of the last one (5 means the start command arrived), and the avatar task's smallest free stack is added to the stack report.
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal_ws_avatar.cpp, hal_head_touch.cpp (MIT). Applies after 065.
# Check after a flash: "unit camera: starts= captured= sent= failed= enc_failed= binary= last_type=" every minute. starts=0 with binary=0: the start never reached the unit; last_type=5, starts>0, captured=0: the capture fails; captured>0, sent=0: the JPEG encode fails; "avatar_ws" under about 1000 in "unit memory" means that task's stack is too small.
